{"causes":["The cultural flourishing of the Islamic Golden Age provided an intellectual backdrop for Rumi's writings.","Rumi's personal experiences with love and loss deeply influenced the themes present in the Masnavi.","The established tradition of Persian poetry and storytelling created a fertile ground for Rumi's innovative poetic form."],"effects":["The Masnavi established Rumi as a central figure in Persian literature and Sufism.","It has influenced countless poets and writers across different cultures and eras.","The text's teachings have contributed to the global understanding of Sufism and Islamic mysticism."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"sufism","sourceName":"Sufism","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"masnavi","targetName":"Masnavi","context":"The Masnavi serves as a foundational text that has shaped the practices and beliefs within Sufism since its publication."},{"sourceSlug":"persian-poetry","sourceName":"Persian Poetry","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"masnavi","targetName":"Masnavi","context":"The Masnavi emerged as a pivotal work within the Persian poetic tradition, influencing styles and themes in subsequent poetry."},{"sourceSlug":"balkh","sourceName":"Balkh, Afghanistan","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"masnavi","targetName":"Masnavi","context":"The Masnavi was written by Rumi, who was born in Balkh, a significant cultural center of the time."},{"sourceSlug":"islamic-golden-age","sourceName":"Islamic Golden Age","verb":"FRAMES","targetSlug":"masnavi","targetName":"Masnavi","context":"The Masnavi is framed within the broader intellectual and cultural achievements of the Islamic Golden Age, which influenced its content."},{"sourceSlug":"james-farrell","sourceName":"James Farrell","verb":"TRANSMITS","targetSlug":"masnavi","targetName":"Masnavi","context":"Scholars like James Farrell have transmitted Rumi's teachings through modern translations that have reached Western audiences."}],"places":[{"name":"Balkh, Afghanistan","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Konya, Turkey","role":"Final residence"},{"name":"Samarkand, Uzbekistan","role":"Cultural center"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Masnavi","type":"Reference article","slug":"masnavi"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q6579646","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Masnavi"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Masnavi contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Masnavi (Persian: مثنوی معنوی, Maṡnavī-ye Maʿnavī, 'Spiritual Couplets') is a six-volume poem in rhyming couplets (masnavi metre) by the Persian Sufi poet and mystic Jalāl al-Dīn Muḥammad Rūmī (1207–1273), composed in Konya (in present-day Turkey) c. 1258–1273 at the instigation of his disciple Husam al-Din Chalabi. Running to approximately 25,000 couplets (51,000 lines) and one of the longest poems in any language, the Masnavi is the summit of Persian Sufi poetry and the work that earned Rumi the title 'Mawlana' (Our Master). It is sometimes called 'the Quran of the Persian language' — a reflection of its central position in Persian literary and spiritual culture.

The Masnavi's structure is associative rather than linear — it moves through stories, parables, Quranic commentary, philosophical discussions, and lyrical passages in a manner that enacts the Sufi understanding of spiritual reality as a web of interconnected meanings rather than a sequential argument. Its opening verses — the 'reed flute' (ney) passage — are among the most famous in all of Persian literature: the image of the reed flute's music as the cry of separation from its origin (the reed bed) as an allegory of the soul's longing for union with the divine is one of the defining metaphors of Sufi thought. The Masnavi contains dozens of embedded stories — many drawn from the Quran, Hadith, Persian folk tradition, and Hindu narratives — that are used as vehicles for Sufi teaching: the story of the reed flute, the elephant in the dark room (a parable for the divine beyond human comprehension), the three fishermen, the lion who hunts alone (on free will and divine help), and hundreds of others.

Rumi's Masnavi has been translated into dozens of languages and is among the best-selling poetry collections in the United States in the late 20th and early 21st centuries (in the translations of Coleman Barks, which, though free paraphrases, introduced millions of English-speaking readers to Sufi mystical poetry). It remains a foundational text of Persian literary culture and of the Sufi tradition worldwide.","subjects":["Persian Literature","Medieval Era","Rumi","Sufi Poetry","Islamic Mysticism","Persian Culture","Epic Poetry","Mystical Literature"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Masnavi has significantly shaped the course of Sufi thought and Persian literature, influencing both Islamic scholarship and global literary traditions, with millions of readers worldwide.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
